<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Carabobo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1821">1821</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venezuelan-war-of-independence">Venezuelan War of Independence</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Carabobo was fought on 24 June 1821 south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, Venezuela. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a>'s patriot army defeated the royalist forces of Miguel de la Torre, with the British <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/legion">Legion</a> playing a notable part. The victory secured Venezuelan independence; the last Spanish garrison held out at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a> until 1823.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-carabobo.jpg" alt="Battle of Carabobo" width="320" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:BatallaCarabobo01.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Venezuela's independence had been won and lost several times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> held <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> from 1810 to 1812 before the Spanish retook it; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> restored the republic with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/admirable-campaign">Admirable Campaign</a> of 1813, lost it again in 1814, and by 1818 had founded a third republic. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colombia">Colombia</a> was formed in 1819 with Bolívar as president, and once it was secure he turned to the liberation of Venezuela. In 1820 he concluded an armistice with the Spanish under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pablo-morillo">Pablo Morillo</a>, and he regrouped his forces and stationed them on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-maracaibo">Lake Maracaibo</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The royalists held the road from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>. Bolívar, with 6,500 or 8,000 men, including some 340 or 350 of the British Rangers battalion, divided his army and sent half on a flanking march through rough, overgrown country under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jose-antonio-paez">José Antonio Páez</a>, while he attacked in the centre. De la Torre split his own force to meet the flank attack, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">musket</a> fire at first threw back the Apure Braves Battalion.</p><p>The British battalion under Colonel Thomas Ilderton Ferrier, which included many former members of the King's German Legion, took the hills and held them though outnumbered and short of supplies; it lost 119 dead, among them 11 officers and Ferrier himself. Reinforced, the patriots pushed the royalists back, and Pedro Camejo was killed rallying the troops in front of Páez. The royalist llanero cavalry fled, the patriot cavalry under Colonel Munoz broke through the centre, and only some 400 men of one royalist infantry regiment reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The victory was costly: the division commanders Ambrosio Plaza and Manuel Cedeño were both killed. Bolívar later called the British legionaries the saviours of his fatherland. With the main royalist army in Venezuela destroyed, independence was assured, and the battle led to the establishment of the Republic of Gran Colombia. The independence forces won a naval victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lake-maracaibo">Battle of Lake Maracaibo</a> on 24 July 1823, and in November 1823 Páez occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-cabello">Puerto Cabello</a>. In Venezuela 24 June is kept as Battle of Carabobo Day, also called Army Day, with a military parade.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Carabobo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Congress of Cúcuta adopted a centralist constitution in 1821, electing Bolívar president and Santander vice-president, and in the same year the <b>Battle of Carabobo</b> freed most of Venezuela.</blockquote><a href="/empire/gran-colombia">Gran Colombia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Captaincy General of Venezuela, birthplace of Simón Bolívar, had been the first Spanish American territory to declare full independence, on 5 July 1811, but its first two republics were crushed by royalist forces, and independence was secured only within Gran Colombia after the <b>Battle of Carabobo</b> in 1821.</blockquote><a href="/empire/venezuela">Venezuela · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Years of war followed until Bolívar's victory at <b>Carabobo</b> in 1821; the last royalists left Puerto Cabello in 1823.</blockquote><a href="/state/captaincy-general-of-venezuela">Captaincy General of Venezuela</a></li></ul></section>
